{
  "fromId": "a",
  "toId": "o",
  "preamble": "The open vowel a is rounded and backed to o. Toda rounded Proto-Dravidian *a and *ā to o and ō in many words (*kaṇ 'eye' > koṇ, *kāl 'leg' > kōl), while neighbouring Kota keeps a (kaṇ, kāl). The best-known case is the Canaanite shift, in which long *ā became ō in Hebrew and the other Canaanite languages (*ḥimār- 'donkey' > ḥămôr, beside Arabic ḥimār; Hebrew šālōm beside Arabic salām). In Albanian, Proto-Albanian long *ā became o (*mā́terā- > motër 'sister').",
  "phoneticEffects": "Rounding, Backing",
  "languageExamples": [
    {
